51-**Part 1 — keep a dedicated CI-tier secret-scanning workflow (high confidence).** `rs-launch-blueprint` ships `.github/workflows/secret-scan.yml`: one job, triggered on `pull_request` and on `push` to `main`, that fails when a live credential is present in the content being scanned. Both engines reached this independently. It survives the strongest contrary evidence in the item — that 0 of 10 surveyed well-regarded Rust projects run such a job (Opus raw, `### Landscape`, workflow inventories retrieved 2026-09-10) — because those projects are all public repositories, where GitHub's native secret scanning is free, whereas a template's principal deployment is a clone into a private repository, where the free tier stops (`https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/introduction/about-secret-scanning`, retrieved 2026-09-10, carried from the Opus raw), and because native scanning raises alerts rather than a pull-request status check that branch protection or R12 can require (same page, same retrieval).
