55:**The gate rule, stated once in full for the tool the ruling selects. Every later mention of the gate in this document carries this paragraph and the one after it by reference.** TruffleHog's documented exit codes are exactly three, and they are **not** Kingfisher's — `0` (no errors and no results), `1` (an error was encountered; sources may not have completed scans), `183` (no errors, but results were found; returned only when `--fail` is passed) — read from `README.md` at the pinned tag `v3.97.4`, from the section headed `Exit Codes:`, and asserted as a set rather than summarised (assertion `06a`; the shipped `--help` documents `--fail` as "Exit with code 183 if results are found", `06b`). **Because `1` is a documented code that means the scan may not have completed, the allow-list of passing codes is `0` and nothing else** — where Kingfisher's rule allowed two codes to pass, this one allows one. The scan step therefore **captures the exit code explicitly and then allow-lists it**: `0` **passes**; `183` **fails the job** (a verified credential was found); `1` **fails the job** (the tool reported an error); and **any code outside that documented set also fails the job as a tool error**, because a code the tool does not document means the tool did not deliver a verdict. Executed: eleven invocations in section 07 produced only `0`, `1` and `183`, and none outside the set (`07k`); an unknown flag exits `1` (`07f`); a binary that is absent altogether does not produce a code of the tool's at all: the value is the *shell's*, and it lands on either side of the documented set depending on which shell ran — a plain shell reports `127`, which is outside it, while bash 3.2 under `errexit` reported `1` in revision 6's section 12, which is **inside** it and is a failing code there anyway. Either way the job fails, and that is the point: allow-listing the one passing code is what makes both outcomes safe, where enumerating failing codes would have to anticipate both.
