243:**Re-verify triggers.** These are rewritten for the tool the owner's ruling selects, and the runner-up's trigger is the reverse of the one it carried in revision 6. (1) `GET https://api.github.com/repos/trufflesecurity/trufflehog` shows `archived: true`, or the CLI stops publishing release binaries for both owner-fixed runners — either flips the recommendation to the **Kingfisher CLI**, and `10a` and `03a` are the assertions that would fail first. (2) P03 measures a live credential that TruffleHog misses and Kingfisher catches — the second half of the runner-up's winning condition. (3) A `3.x` release that rejects `--only-verified`, which is a **hidden** flag at this pin (`06d`, `06e`) — the spelling in the owner's ruling then becomes `--results=verified`, which is a flag change and not a tool change, and `06e` is the assertion that would catch it. (4) TruffleHog begins publishing a GitHub build attestation — `04g` then **fails**, and the install step simplifies to `gh attestation verify` with the prerequisites the provenance paragraphs name. (5) The documented exit-code set changes at a later tag — `06a` fails and the allow-list is re-derived at the new pin. (6) GitHub makes secret scanning free on private repositories **and** adds a pull-request status check — together those would make architecture A viable and could retire the workflow; either alone would not. (7) R39 selects TruffleHog for the hook tier — then the `BASELINE-REVIEW: F050` line must be resolved by the owner before both tiers share a tool. (8) R11 or R12 constrains the scan job in a way that assumes a first-party Action or a specific runner OS.
